.TH RIG 1 "2026-10-03" "Knowledge Islands" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
\&
.PP
rig \- describe and manage a personal working setup
.SH SYNOPSIS
\&
.br
.B rig init
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
.br
.B rig repair
.RI [ \-\-output " PATH" ]
.br
.B rig show
.RI [ ITEM ]
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-all ]
.RI [ \-\-category " ID" ]
.RI [ \-\-format " text|json" ]
.br
.B rig status
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-problems ]
.RI [ \-\-unmanaged ]
.RI [ \-\-retired ]
.RI [ \-\-format " text|json" ]
.br
.B rig capture
.RI [ ITEM... ]
.RI [ \-\-provider " homebrew" ]
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
.RI [ \-\-output " PATH" ]
.RI [ \-\-category " ID" ]
.RI [ \-\-purpose " TEXT" ]
.RI [ \-\-rationale " TEXT" ]
.br
.B rig apply
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-scope " tools|skills|resources|all" ]
.RI [ \-\-target " ID" ]...
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
.RI [ \-\-format " text|json" ]
.br
.B rig upgrade
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
.RI [ \-\-unattended ]
.RI [ \-\-format " text|json" ]
.br
.B rig doctor
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-verbose ]
.RI [ \-\-format " text|json" ]
.br
.B rig diag
.RI [ \-\-full ]
.br
.BI "rig export \-\-profile " NAME
.BI " \-\-output " DIRECTORY
.RI [ \-\-title " TEXT" ]
.RI [ \-\-base-url " URL" ]
.br
.BI "rig completion " bash|zsh
.br
.B rig help
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.br
.B rig
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\&
.PP
Rig describes a person's tools, user-level skills, and managed resources,
explains why they belong, and compares selected intent with the machine.
Managed resources include services, scheduled jobs, typed macOS settings,
and semantic Dock layouts. Profiles select declarations for machines, roles,
or contexts. Built-in providers materialise them; explicit external providers
extend the built-in set.
.PP
Rig configuration owns package intent. Homebrew, uv, mise, npm, and other
providers retain native resolution, execution, credentials, and observed
state. ChezMoi retains its native source, templates, and configuration
authority. No Brewfile is required or used by Rig.
.PP
Show parses inert configuration without invoking providers. Status and doctor
observe native state. Apply reconciles selected declarations after preflight;
upgrade advances selected supported tools. Init creates configuration.
Capture discovers supported installations and prepares additive declarations
for review; it never automatically adopts observed state. Export writes public
data without invoking a network command.
.SH USER WORKFLOW
\&
.PP
Use
.B rig init
to create a minimal default configuration without discovering or installing
software. Add meaningful categories and declarations.
.B rig show
describes the selected setup;
.B rig show \-\-all
browses the catalogue; and
.BI "rig show " ITEM
explains a declaration.
.PP
Use
.B rig doctor
for health,
.B rig doctor \-\-verbose
for runtime/configuration diagnostics, and
.B rig status
to compare intent with observations.
.B \-\-unmanaged
includes supported inventory beyond the catalogue.
.PP
For software installed outside Rig, discover it with
.BR "rig capture" ,
select an observed identity, supply its category, purpose, and rationale,
then review the proposed TOML before manually adopting it.
Capture does not infer why a tool belongs or remove intent absent from this
machine.
.PP
Review
.B rig apply \-\-dry-run
before native changes. Apply handles initial setup and later reconciliation,
including declared prerequisites. Use
.B rig upgrade
to advance selected tools, and native providers for housekeeping, logs,
restarts, and other imperative actions.
.SH INSTALLATION
\&
.PP
Install the latest immutable release, currently v0.4.0:
.PP
.nf
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/knowledgeislands/tools-rig/v0.4.0/install.sh | bash
.fi
.PP
Pin an exact release explicitly:
.PP
.nf
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/knowledgeislands/tools-rig/v0.4.0/install.sh | bash -s -- v0.4.0
.fi
.PP
These URLs name the latest documented immutable release. From a local
checkout, run
.B ./install.sh \-\-link
to link both the executable and manual; the linked executable reports a
development version marker until a release candidate is explicitly selected.
.PP
The installer writes the executable to
.I ~/.local/bin
and the manual beneath
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/man/man1
by default. Set
.B RIG_INSTALL_DIR
or
.B RIG_MAN_INSTALL_DIR
to choose different locations. Set
.B RIG_VERSION
to an exact
.B vX.Y.Z
release for automation compatibility. A positional version takes precedence.
Without either form, the installer discovers the latest GitHub release.
.SH COMMANDS
\&
.TP
.B rig init
Create minimal unversioned configuration and a complete default profile at
.IR RIG_CONFIG_HOME/rig.toml ,
using the XDG default when the override is absent. Existing root configuration,
fragments, and symlinked configuration targets are refused.
.B \-\-dry-run
prints the target and content without creating directories or files.
.TP
.B rig repair
For a valid legacy configuration with
.BR "schema = 1" ,
the default invocation previews removal of that marker.
.B \-\-output
writes a repaired proposal to a new file outside active configuration for
review and manual replacement; it never rewrites an active source.
.TP
.B rig show
Describe the default resolved profile with tools, skills, and resources.
.BI "\-\-profile " NAME
selects a named profile without changing the default.
.TP
.B rig show \-\-all
Browse all catalogue tools in deterministic order.
.BI "\-\-category " ID
filters tools by category, optionally combined with
.B \-\-profile
to restrict them to that profile.
The
.B \-\-all
and
.B \-\-profile
options are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.BI "rig show " TOOL|skill:ID|service:ID|scheduled-job:ID|setting:ID|dock:ID|port:ID
Explain identity, purpose, rationale, installation metadata, relationships,
native authority, and profile membership as applicable. Dock explanations
include ordered items; ports include TCP number, bind scope, mode, and owner.
Item explanations accept only the format option, not profile/category/all
selection options.
.PP
.BR show ,
.BR apply ,
and
.B upgrade
accept
.BI "\-\-format " text|json .
JSON retains lossless structured values; text columns wrap descriptive values.
Mutation JSON
groups complete rows by named columns; diagnostics remain on stderr.
Capture instead prints human discovery or reviewable TOML, without a format
option.
.TP
.B rig status
Compare every tool, user-level skill, and operational resource in the
resolved default profile
with its selected native observation. Catalogue-only tools have their own
neutral state rather than counting as unavailable; stale resource receipts
are pending retirement.
Selected historical apply failures appear separately from these current
observations, with their completion time, age and native exit status. An
installed tool may remain present while its last completed apply failed.
History findings contribute exit status 1 without changing native state
counters; they remain visible with
.BR \-\-problems .
JSON includes a separate
.B apply_failures
array and historical count. Doctor reports the same evidence.
Same-provider declaration changes retain historical evidence, not a claim
about the changed declaration. A later successful apply clears the matching
failure; unrelated applies do not. Missing history is neutral. Damaged,
unreadable or unsafe history is an unavailable-history finding, and future
timestamps are clock discrepancies rather than negative ages.
When running without
.BR HOME ,
set
.B RIG_STATE_HOME
or
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
explicitly; an unresolved history location is unavailable evidence, not
missing history.
The report opens with a verdict line naming how many entries need attention
and the sections holding them, and every section closes with its own summary
counters.
Human output uses aligned, ruled tables with two-space column gutters.
Interactive tables use the actual terminal width and wrap descriptive values.
If columns cannot fit, entries use a labelled, stacked layout.
Identifiers remain intact, even when an unusually long identifier exceeds
terminal width. Redirected
output is plain and deterministic with a 120-column budget, independent of
ambient COLUMNS. Unavailable terminal geometry uses the same fallback.
Human cells render terminal controls as visible escapes, never executable
terminal sequences. UTF-8 code points stay intact, with a conservative two-cell
budget per non-ASCII code point; combining marks and joined emoji may underfill
rows. A wide glyph on a one-cell terminal remains intact even when it exceeds
the budget. JSON retains original values.
The spacing is not a machine-readable interface.
A declared private port compares its owner against the whole command line of
the bound process, so a listener started through an interpreter still matches
the service program or tool locator it was declared to run.
.B conflicting
asserts that a readable command line identifies a different process, and names
that process. An unreadable command line whose executable name does not match
is reported as
.B unknown
with
.BR owner-unavailable ,
or remains informational for an allocated port; it is never reported as a
conflict.
.TP
.BI "rig status \-\-profile " NAME
Compare a named resolved profile without changing the configured default.
.TP
.B rig status \-\-problems
Report only the entries that need attention, omitting present rows and any
section left empty by that filter. A catalogue-only tool needs nobody, so it
is not an entry that needs attention. The verdict line, the per-section
summary counters, and the exit status are unchanged, and
.B \-\-format json
still carries the complete payload.
.TP
.B rig status \-\-unmanaged
Also ask a directly installed Skills CLI for global skill identities not
represented by a matching declaration. An absent, failing, unsupported, or
malformed inventory is reported unavailable. KI inventory remains unavailable
and is never invoked.
On macOS, also report read-only TCP listener observations whose port is not
declared. Listener inventory is private and informational; it never opens,
reserves, closes, or kills a socket.
Additionally ask each selected inventory source to enumerate its native
domain, and report each observed identity that no tool installation declares.
Built-in inventory needs no configuration declaration; external inventory
must be explicitly allowed. Unmanaged rows are informational and do not make
the result unhealthy. Reverse reconciliation answers the opposite question to
the tool table: not whether declared software is installed, but whether
installed software was ever declared.
Homebrew inventory covers formulae installed on request and installed casks;
dependency-only formulae are excluded. Matching uses declared provider
identities across all profiles, rather than only the current selection.
The macOS application scanner remains an additional read-only source.
.TP
.B rig status \-\-retired
Include private, informational evidence for every
.B [retired-application.ID]
declaration in the loaded configuration. Profile selection still governs the
ordinary machine comparison; it does not filter retired declarations.
.B \-\-problems
does not hide explicitly requested retirement evidence. Retained data and
unavailable evidence do not alter health counts, doctor findings or exit status.
No cleanup, installation or removal is performed.
.PP
Observe only declared application locations, explicit data and package paths,
and exact bundle-ID candidates under Library Application Support, Caches,
Preferences, Saved Application State and Containers. A bundle matches only
through its exact CFBundleIdentifier. Missing, inaccessible, mismatched,
symlink and unavailable evidence remain distinct. No path-component symlink
is followed and no data contents or recursive directory inventory is read.
Declared package association and conventional candidates are not verified
ownership. Non-macOS platforms report unavailable evidence without native
probes.
.PP
Optional last-used metadata comes from Spotlight for an exactly matched bundle.
It is partial evidence, not proof of use or disuse; unavailable metadata does
not mean never used. Normal status does not probe retired paths.
.B \-\-retired
adds a private
.B retired_applications
JSON array, whose evidence paths remain in
.BR detail .
.TP
.BI "rig status \-\-format " text|json
Select the rendering. The default
.B text
prints the tables described above.
.B json
prints one JSON object on a single line on standard output once observation
has finished, projecting the same observation the tables render, so the two
can never disagree about a state, a count, or a verdict. The exit status is
unchanged, and the payload carries
.B healthy
and the summary counts so a consumer never has to read it. The envelope
carries an integer
.BR schema ,
the running
.BR rig " version, the " command ,
the resolved
.BR profile " and " platform ", and " observed_at .
.B rig status
adds
.BR tools ", " skills ", " resources ", and " ports
arrays naming each item's identity, owner, kind, state, and detail, with
.BR unmanaged " and " unmanaged_problems
left null unless
.B \-\-unmanaged
was requested.
A field named
.BR detail ", a " findings " entry, or an " information " entry"
is human-facing text and is the only place a local path may appear; no other
field carries one. Progress and native provider diagnostics remain on standard
error. Any other value is rejected with status 2 before anything is observed,
and a change that removes or repurposes a field increments
.BR schema .
.TP
.B rig doctor
Check the resolved default profile and print one compact operational health
answer. Doctor checks effective XDG path accessibility, native-manager and
external-provider availability, and selected tool, skill, and resource
observations.
Common Tool, Version, Installation, runtime-host Platform, Architecture, Runtime
and Configuration fields accompany a healthy or unhealthy Verdict, Coverage,
and pass/warn/fail/skipped Checks. Counts use evaluated items, not subprocesses:
configuration, each XDG directory, history availability, selected declarations,
retirement and historical-failure items. Dependent selection is one skipped
group when configuration cannot load. Catalogue-only and incompatible items are
skipped. Warning counts are currently zero. This is read-only declared setup
health; available package updates are explicitly not checked.
Catalogue-only and
incompatible-platform tools are informational. An observed competing
auto-update agent Rig did not declare, such as Homebrew's
.BR "brew autoupdate" ,
is reported as information alone: it adds no finding, changes no exit status,
and Rig never installs, modifies, or removes it.
.TP
.B rig diag [\-\-full]
Print offline, share-safe Tool, Version, Installation, Platform, Architecture,
Runtime and Configuration facts, matching doctor's context.
Runtime host platform
is separate from the selected provider platform. Installation is local for a
proven checkout payload, release for a Homebrew Cellar payload with a receipt,
or unknown when provenance is unavailable; version alone is not proof.
Root or fragment configuration presence does not parse its contents. Use
.B \-\-full
to include executable and effective XDG paths; review full output before sharing
it. Configuration values and secrets are never printed.
.TP
.BI "rig doctor \-\-profile " NAME
Check a named resolved profile without changing the configured default.
Doctor invokes only fixed built-in observation operations or external
observation operations explicitly allowed for selected providers. It never
applies, repairs, or invokes unselected providers.
When configuration cannot load, doctor reports its file, line, reason, and
correction as a finding with status 1. It still checks effective paths,
platform, and native provider executable availability, but skips checks
requiring a resolved catalogue. Other operational commands still reject the
configuration with status 2.
.TP
.BI "rig doctor \-\-format " text|json
Select the rendering. The default
.B text
prints the tables described above.
.B json
prints one JSON object on a single line on standard output once observation
has finished, projecting the same observation the tables render, so the two
can never disagree about a state, a count, or a verdict. The exit status is
unchanged, and the payload carries
.B healthy
and the summary counts so a consumer never has to read it. The envelope
carries an integer
.BR schema ,
the running
.BR rig " version, the " command ,
the resolved
.BR profile " and " platform ", and " observed_at .
.B rig doctor
adds its
.B findings
grouped by origin and its
.BR information .
A field named
.BR detail ", a " findings " entry, or an " information " entry"
is human-facing text and is the only place a local path may appear; no other
field carries one. Progress and native provider diagnostics remain on standard
error. Any other value is rejected with status 2 before anything is observed,
and a change that removes or repurposes a field increments
.BR schema .
.TP
.B rig apply
Preflight every selected bound tool, operational resource, stale receipt
entry, and receipt target before invoking providers. Tools run in
dependency-first order, followed by selected skills, selected resources, and
stale retirement.
A failure skips only dependent work; independent work continues. A
resource-local preflight finding produces a failed row and prevents only that
resource from being invoked. Shared configuration, trust, executable,
platform, and receipt-boundary failures stop the plan before mutation. The
atomic resource receipt advances only after the complete resource plan
succeeds, and stale retirement is withheld after any selected resource
failure. The report identifies declaration scope before execution.
Receipt-backed work acquires one exclusive
platform lock before reading the receipt. A profile whose kind is
.B view
is non-appliable and is rejected before mutation.
.B \-\-target
narrows the fully resolved profile to exact selected entries; an unknown ID
fails with status 2 before dispatch. A targeted run does not retire unrelated
stale resources, records only reconciled resources in its receipt, and labels
requested versus dependency-included rows. A port target selects its declared
owner; Rig never materialises the port itself.
.PP
Apply also stages selected declarations that explicitly provide missing native
managers. Required Homebrew-to-mise and mise-to-Node/npm prerequisites must be
in the selected dependency closure; Rig does not infer unselected tools.
Configuration, trust, and shared safety checks complete before the first
mutation, and downstream executable availability is rechecked after each
prerequisite stage. It does not use a Brewfile or configure Homebrew autoupdate.
.TP
.BI "rig apply \-\-profile " NAME
Apply a named resolved profile without changing the configured default.
.TP
.B rig apply \-\-dry-run
Perform complete preflight and print planned or resource-local failed work
without invoking a provider mutation.
.TP
.BI "rig apply \-\-target " ID
Reconcile one selected tool ID, or a qualified
.BR skill:ID ,
.BR service:ID ,
.BR scheduled-job:ID ,
.BR setting:ID ,
.BR dock:ID ,
or
.BR port:ID .
Repeat the option for several exact targets. Missing prerequisites are included
and labelled as dependencies; already-present prerequisites are omitted. A
.B \-\-scope
that excludes a missing prerequisite fails before dispatch.
Use
.B \-\-dry-run
to inspect the bounded plan first.
.TP
.BI "rig apply \-\-scope " tools|skills|resources|all
Limit reconciliation to selected tool installations, selected user-level skills,
managed resources and their stale receipts, or all three. The default is
.BR all .
The scope changes execution, not profile resolution or declaration ownership.
.TP
.B rig upgrade
Advance selected tools and user-level skills through fixed Homebrew, uv, mise,
npm, and Skills CLI native operations, deduplicated by selected installation.
.B \-\-profile
selects a complete profile.
Unsupported providers are skipped; unavailable executables produce unavailable
rows while independent work continues, returning 1.
Runtime, plugin, KI, and local skill authorities are not advanced.
.B \-\-dry-run
performs resolution and preflight without provider mutation.
Unknown packages and skills are never removed.
.RS
.PP
.B \-\-unattended
states that nobody is watching. Provider stdin comes from
.IR /dev/null ,
Homebrew receives
.BR NONINTERACTIVE=1 ,
and selected App Store upgrades are unavailable with
.B interactive-required
before invocation. Other native failures retain their exit detail while
independent work continues. Native programs may still interact through a
terminal, graphical authentication, or credential helper; this flag is not a
timeout or process supervisor. A non-dry run writes the
.I last-upgrade
report described under
.BR FILES .
.RE
.TP
.B rig capture
Discover Homebrew formulae explicitly installed on request and all installed
casks. Dependency-only formulae are excluded; casks do not claim equivalent
installation-reason filtering. Discovery is read-only and reports declared,
unmanaged, or ambiguous-declaration identities.
.B \-\-provider
currently accepts only
.BR homebrew ,
also the default.
.TP
.BI "rig capture " ITEM...
Select an unambiguous locator or qualified
.B formula:LOCATOR
or
.B cask:LOCATOR
from installed inventory. Absent or ambiguous selections fail before writing.
Proposals require an existing
.B \-\-category
and explicit
.B \-\-purpose
and
.BR \-\-rationale .
Without all three, capture only describes the selected inventory.
.B \-\-profile
sets proposed membership, defaulting to the configured default complete
profile. Central membership lists must first be converted to item-owned
membership, or adoption performed manually; discovery remains available.
.RS
.PP
Complete metadata produces additive TOML on stdout.
.BI "\-\-output " PATH
creates a new review file outside active configuration and its fragment
directory, including symlink aliases; its parent must exist.
Existing files and symlinks are never overwritten.
.B \-\-dry-run
prints the proposal without writing that file, while still performing
read-only native discovery.
.PP
Review and deliberately copy the fragment into
.I conf.d
to adopt it. Capture never writes active configuration, changes native
manifests, installs software, or removes declarations absent on this machine.
It deduplicates provider identities across all profiles. Ambiguous tap aliases
and generated tool-ID collisions require manual resolution. Human purpose,
rationale, category, and profile intent are not inferred.
Settings and other providers are outside this initial capture capability.
.RE
.TP
.BI "rig export \-\-profile " NAME " \-\-output " DIRECTORY
Generate a deterministic, versioned
.I rig.json
artifact from the named platform-neutral view profile. Export invokes no
provider and no network command.
.RS
.PP
.B \-\-profile
must name a declared profile whose
.B kind
is
.BR view ;
an appliable profile is rejected before anything is written.
.B \-\-title
states the title the exported document carries, defaulting to the view's
declared
.B name
and then to the profile identifier.
.B \-\-base-url
states the canonical URL of the published document; omitting it renders
.B canonical_url
as
.BR null .
Rig accepts none of these from configuration: they belong to the site that
consumes the export.
.RE
.TP
.B rig doctor \-\-verbose
Include runtime/configuration evidence with normal health checks: Rig and Bash
versions, invoked executable, platform, XDG directories, root configuration,
fragment count, validity, default profile,
selection mode, and
declaration counts. JSON embeds the snapshot as
.B diagnostics
while retaining ordinary findings and exit status.
.TP
.BI "rig completion " bash|zsh
Print completion source for the selected shell. With
.B \-h
or
.BR \-\-help ,
print command usage without generating completion source.
.TP
.B rig help
Show the compact top-level command summary, or use
.BI "rig help " COMMAND
for detailed command syntax. The optional
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
forms produce the same output.
.SH MIGRATION
\&
.PP
The reduced surface has no compatibility aliases. Replace
.B list
with
.BR "show \-\-all" ,
.BI "explain " ITEM
with
.BI "show " ITEM ,
.B diag
with
.BR "doctor \-\-verbose" ,
.B bootstrap
with
.BR apply ,
and
.B update
with
.BR upgrade .
The old
.B run
and
.B maintain
commands have no generic replacement: use native providers for imperative
actions and housekeeping.
.PP
Remove
.B [rig] bootstrap-profile
and use
.B apply \-\-profile NAME
with explicit prerequisites. Remove Homebrew
.BR manifest ,
.BR autoupdate-interval ,
and
.B autoupdate-options
from Rig configuration. Declare selected package installations in Rig; retain
or retire any independently used Brewfile through its owner's workflow.
The old manifest-snapshot capture is replaced by reviewed Rig declaration
proposals.
.PP
Review existing scheduler commands and switch intended upgrade jobs to
.BR "rig upgrade \-\-unattended" .
Rig writes new outcomes to
.I last-upgrade
and does not delete an existing
.I last-update
report. No migration automatically changes chezmoi source, templates, or
host-owned schedules.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
\&
.PP
The
.B rig completion bash
and
.B rig completion zsh
print completion definitions for the shipped command and option surface.
.PP
For Bash, source the definition from the shell configuration:
.PP
.nf
source <(rig completion bash)
.fi
.PP
For Zsh, write the definition to a file named
.I _rig
in a directory on
.B fpath
before
.B compinit
runs. For example:
.PP
.nf
mkdir -p ~/.zsh/completions
rig completion zsh > ~/.zsh/completions/_rig
fpath=(~/.zsh/completions $fpath)
autoload -Uz compinit && compinit
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
\&
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Show command help.
.TP
.BR \-V , " \-\-version"
Print the Rig release or development version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\&
.PP
Rig reads an optional
.I ${RIG_CONFIG_HOME}/rig.toml
first when present and then regular
.I ${RIG_CONFIG_HOME}/conf.d/*.toml
fragments in bytewise filename order. At least one source must exist, and the
merged declaration must contain exactly one
.B [rig]
table.
When
.B RIG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, its default is
.IR ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig .
.PP
Configuration uses a bounded TOML grammar: named tables, an optional
recognised legacy integer schema marker, single-line basic strings,
bounded single-line or multiline
basic-string arrays, blank lines, and comments. Accepted files are valid TOML.
Rig rejects unused TOML features
and does not source configuration, evaluate commands, interpret shell syntax,
or perform general environment expansion.
.SS ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
\&
.TP
.B RIG_CONFIG_HOME
Override the Rig configuration directory.
.TP
.B RIG_DATA_HOME
Override the Rig data directory.
.TP
.B RIG_STATE_HOME
Override the Rig state directory.
.TP
.B RIG_CACHE_HOME
Override the Rig cache directory.
.TP
.B RIG_PLATFORM
Override the active catalogue platform used by profile and installation
resolution.
By default Rig maps Bash
.B OSTYPE
values beginning with
.B darwin
to
.B macos
and values beginning with
.B linux
to
.BR linux .
.TP
.B RIG_PROGRESS
Set to
.B always
to force progress, including declaration-only queries. A supported interactive
terminal receives a two-line bottom footer for Rig-owned phases and single
native-capable items, showing the command, resolved selection (or selection
pending), phase, completed/total work, current target and phase-local outcome
counts. Multi-item native phases use compact target-and-scope handoff headers
with a one-based ordinal and completed count, without reserving footer rows.
Routine item successes are quiet; skips, failures and a final phase summary
remain visible. Redirected stderr receives stable line-oriented events.
Rig releases the terminal before native execution.
Native output may scroll the handoff header away.
Set to
.B lines
to request those durable events even on a terminal, or
.B never
to suppress it. The default is
.BR auto ,
which selects the footer for owned phases and single native-capable items on
supported terminals, compact handoffs for native batches, and line-oriented
events otherwise; declaration-only
queries remain quiet.
Unsupported or dumb terminals, unavailable geometry and terminals smaller than
eight rows or sixty columns use line events. Counts are not a duration estimate.
Enumerable phases report a running item without advancing the completed count,
then advance once on a succeeded, skipped, or failed outcome. Consequential
work names its declaration scope before invocation.
Complete, failure-free footer phases leave no permanent success summary,
including phases with skipped work. The configuration phases share one footer
reservation instead of accumulating blank rows. Failed, interrupted, incomplete
and failed-item phases retain their summaries, as do line-output phases.
Failure and interruption terminate the active phase explicitly. Rig emits a
durable task header and releases the terminal before native execution. Native
prompts and diagnostics are not buffered or filtered. A separating newline
protects native output without a final newline before Rig resumes reporting.
Resize is handled only
at safe rendering boundaries. Reflowed footer text may remain in scrollback:
Rig does not erase uncertain coordinates at the risk of losing diagnostics.
No alternate screen, cursor hiding or background
animation is used. Completion, failure and handled interruption restore the
terminal; uncatchable termination cannot guarantee cleanup.
Rig progress omits private paths, locators,
arguments, environment
values, titles, observed details, credentials, and native output. Progress
never changes the deterministic report on stdout.
.TP
.B RIG_OUTCOME
Control the outcome line Rig writes to stderr as the last thing a command
emits. The default is
.BR auto ,
which states the outcome when stderr is a terminal. Set to
.B always
to state it whenever stderr is redirected as well, or to
.B never
to suppress it. The line has the fixed shape
.BI "rig: " "command result" ": status " n
followed by an optional parenthesised detail clause naming the counts or the
finding that decided the status.
.I result
is one of
.BR succeeded ,
.BR healthy ,
.BR unhealthy ,
.BR incomplete ,
or
.BR failed .
A status 2 rejection is not restated, because
.B rig: error:
has already named its cause; neither are
.BR help ,
.BR completion ,
nor
.BR \-\-version ,
which report on Rig rather than on a machine. The line never reaches stdout and
never changes an exit status.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Set the base configuration directory when
.B RIG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Set the base data directory when
.B RIG_DATA_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.local/share .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Set the base state directory when
.B RIG_STATE_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.local/state .
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Set the base cache directory when
.B RIG_CACHE_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.cache .
.SS CONFIGURATION TABLES
\&
.SS CATALOGUE AND PROFILES
\&
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig/rig.toml
Optional root configuration file. The current input shape uses
.B [rig]
with
.B default-profile
and an optional recognised legacy
.B schema = 1
marker;
.B [category.ID]
with
.B name
and
.BR purpose ;
.B [tool.ID]
with
.BR name ,
.BR category ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
and a non-empty
.B platforms
string array, plus optional
.BR requires ,
.BR related ,
.BR alternatives ,
and
.B artifacts
string arrays; and
.B [skill.ID]
requires
.BR name ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
.BR authority ,
.BR source ,
.BR trust ,
and a non-empty
.B platforms
string array. Optional fields are
.BR source-skill ,
.BR runtimes ,
.BR requires ,
.BR profiles ,
and reviewed HTTP or HTTPS
.BR public-source .
Authorities
.BR skills-cli ,
.BR ki ,
and
.B local
require reviewed trust;
.B runtime
and
.B plugin
require authority-owned trust.
.B [profile.ID]
may declare
.BR name ,
.BR purpose ,
.B kind
as
.B complete
or
.BR view ,
and an
.B inherits
string array. Each selectable tool or resource may declare a
.B profiles
string array beside its own fields. Omission assigns that declaration to
.BR default-profile ;
an explicitly empty array assigns it to no profile. Central profile member
arrays remain accepted only when the merged configuration contains no
item-owned membership. Tool
.B install.*
fields retain their native locator and argument data without executing it while
configuration is loaded. Installation metadata is valid only in its owning
tool table.
.SS RETIRED APPLICATIONS
\&
.TP
.B [retired-application.ID]
Declare a private observation target, separate from tools and profiles.
Required fields are
.BR name ", " bundle-id ", and a non-empty " application-paths
string array. Optional
.BR data-paths " and " package-paths
arrays name explicit retained locations. Bundle identifiers must be exact,
dot-separated identities and unique across retired declarations. Paths must
be literal absolute paths or begin with
.BR ~/ " or " $HOME/ .
Shell/glob syntax and dot/parent traversal components are rejected.
Installation and profile fields are not accepted. Apply, upgrade and public
export ignore retired declarations. An independent active tool declaration
still expresses its own installation intent; retirement metadata does not
override it. Removing a retired declaration changes reporting only.
.PP
The optional native metadata executable override
.B RIG_MDLS
defaults to
.IR /usr/bin/mdls ;
bundle identity uses the existing
.B RIG_PLUTIL
override or
.IR /usr/bin/plutil .
.SS MANAGED RESOURCES
\&
.B [service.ID]
and
.B [scheduled-job.ID]
declare operational resources. Both require
.BR name ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
.BR provider ,
.BR locator ,
.BR desired-state ,
non-empty
.B platforms
and
.B program
arrays, and accept
.BR requires ,
.BR environment ,
.BR working-directory ,
.BR standard-output ,
.BR standard-error ,
and
.BR associated-applications .
The last is a string array of application bundle identifiers; on macOS it
renders
.B AssociatedBundleIdentifiers
so the resource is presented under that application's name and icon in
System Settings, Login Items & Extensions, rather than under its program's file
name.
macOS honours the request only for an agent whose own program carries a code
signature; an unsigned program, such as a shell script, is recorded with no
association and no developer, whatever bundle identifier the property list
names.
Services accept
.B restart-policy
and
.BR start-policy .
Scheduled jobs require exactly one of
.B schedule.calendar
or
.B schedule.interval
and accept
.B run-policy
and
.BR priority .
Profiles select resources with
.B services
and
.B scheduled-jobs
arrays. Resource requirements name tools. Every managed resource may also
declare a
.B depends-on
string array containing qualified
.BR service:ID ,
.BR scheduled-job:ID ,
.BR setting:ID ,
or
.B dock:ID
references. Rig rejects missing references and cycles, selects dependencies
transitively, and orders resources deterministically before provider work.
.PP
.B [setting.ID]
declares one typed macOS defaults value. It requires
.BR name ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
.BR provider ,
.BR domain ,
.BR key ,
.BR value-type ,
.BR value ,
and a non-empty
.B platforms
array, and accepts
.BR requires .
The provider is the built-in
.BR macos-defaults ;
.B value-type
is
.BR bool ,
.BR int ,
.BR float ,
or
.BR string .
.PP
String setting values and Dock paths resolve exact whole-value
.BR ~ ,
.BR ~/... ,
.BR $HOME ,
and
.B $HOME/...
forms. String settings additionally resolve exact
.B file://$HOME
and
.B file://$HOME/...
forms. Rig does not expand embedded variables, other variable names,
unsupported tilde forms, or shell syntax. Queries retain the authored value;
observation, preflight, and application use the resolved value.
.PP
.B [dock.ID]
declares one semantic macOS Dock order. It requires
.BR name ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
.BR provider ,
and non-empty
.B platforms
and
.B items
arrays, and accepts
.BR requires .
The provider is the built-in
.BR macos-dock .
Each ordered item names one
.B [dock-item.ID]
table with
.B kind
and
.BR path .
Kind
.B application
names an application path. Kind
.B folder
may also declare its
.B view
and
.B display
semantics. Profiles select settings and Dock layouts through their respective
arrays; Dock items are selected only by a Dock layout.
.PP
.B [port.ID]
declares one private TCP allocation. It requires
.BR name ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
.BR "protocol = \(dqtcp\(dq" ,
a decimal
.B port
from 1 through 65535,
.B scope
set to
.B loopback
or
.BR all-interfaces ,
.B mode
set to
.BR required ,
.BR on-demand ,
or
.BR allocated ,
and an
.B owner
qualified as
.BR tool:ID ,
.BR service:ID ,
or
.BR scheduled-job:ID .
It accepts item-owned
.B profiles
membership. Omitted membership selects the configured default profile; an
empty array selects no profile. Port declarations are observation-only and
never enter apply, receipt, or public projection data.
\&
.SS PROVIDERS AND INSTALLATIONS
\&
.PP
Provider identities built into Rig need no
.B [provider.ID]
table and their supported operations cannot be granted or removed by
configuration. The built-in identities are
.BR homebrew ,
.BR uv ,
.BR mise ,
.BR npm ,
.BR chezmoi ,
.BR direct-download ,
.BR launchd ,
.BR macos-applications ,
.BR macos-defaults ,
and
.BR macos-dock .
Where documented, a built-in provider table may override its native
.B executable
and literal
.BR arguments ;
it cannot declare an adapter or capabilities.
Homebrew
.BR manifest ,
.BR autoupdate-interval ,
and
.B autoupdate-options
are retired and rejected with migration advice. Declare package installations
in Rig and schedule selected upgrades explicitly. ChezMoi retains its native
source and templates.
.PP
An identity not built into Rig is an external provider. Its
.B [provider.ID]
table requires a non-empty
.B capabilities
operation allow-list, and accepts an
.B executable
and literal
.BR arguments .
If
.B executable
is omitted, Rig resolves exactly
.IR ${RIG_DATA_HOME}/providers/ID .
Rig does not search for alternatives or execute adjacent files.
.B [tool.ID]
requires
.B install.provider
when materialised, together with
.B install.kind
and
.B install.locator
and accepts optional scalar
.B install.destination
and
.B install.checksum
fields plus
.B install.platforms
and
.B install.arguments
string arrays. A tool with no install provider is catalogue-only. Destination
and checksum are restricted to direct-download installations.
.PP
The optional tool
.B artifacts
array keeps durable generated paths with the capability that owns them. Rig
observes these paths through
.B status
and
.BR doctor .
For macOS application bundles, a healthy artifact has a readable regular
.I Contents/Info.plist
and at least one entry resolving to a regular executable beneath a real
.I Contents/MacOS
directory. Artifacts are observation-only; their native tool remains
responsible for creation, update, and removal.
.PP
A declared artifact that is a symbolic link is observed through the target it
resolves to, so a command line an application installs into a shared executable
directory can be declared. Resolution follows at most 40 leaf links and
resolves a relative target against the link's own directory. The resolved
target must satisfy the same tests as a directly declared path, so a dangling
link is
.BR missing ,
a link into a damaged application bundle is
.BR drifted ,
and a target that is neither a regular file nor a directory stays
.BR unavailable .
A link Rig cannot resolve, because it cycles or exhausts the bound, is
.B unavailable
with a detail naming failed resolution. Where a link was followed, the detail
reports the resolved target beside the declared path. Rig never creates,
repairs, or repoints a link.
.PP
One tool may declare bounded platform variants through dotted
.B variant.ID.*
fields. Every variant requires a non-empty
.B variant.ID.platforms
array and may contain
.B variant.ID.artifacts
and a complete
.BR variant.ID.install.provider ,
.BR variant.ID.install.kind ,
.B variant.ID.install.locator
group with the corresponding optional installation fields. Every platform
declared by the tool must match exactly one variant. Rig rejects uncovered or
overlapping variants and never includes their installation or artifact data in
a public export.
.SS PUBLICATION AND EXTENSIONS
\&
.B [publication.ID]
is retired. Its values are now arguments of
.BR "rig export" ,
and a configuration that still declares one fails to load.
.B [action.PROVIDER.NAME]
requires
.BR mode ,
and
.BR description .
It accepts
.BR platforms ,
.BR arguments ,
and
.BR allowed-arguments ,
and optional
.B argument-policy
and
.B resource-kinds
fields. Resource-aware actions require provider argument policy and accept one
selected qualified resource before any caller arguments. The mode is exactly
.B observe
or
.BR mutate .
These action declarations retain schema validation for existing extension
configuration, but there is no public Rig action-dispatch command.
Use the native executable for imperative operations.
Argument policy defaults to Rig's exact allowlist; value
.B provider
delegates validation to that external provider.
.PP
The
.B \-\-base-url
argument of
.B rig export
must be an absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL without a query or fragment. Rig appends
a trailing slash when omitted and projects it as
.B publication.canonical_url
metadata. Rig does not generate presentation or navigation.
\&
.SS MINIMAL EXAMPLE
\&
.PP
A minimal catalogue is:
.nf
[rig]
default-profile = "default"

[category.navigation]
name = "Navigation"
purpose = "Move through Knowledge Islands"

[tool.mgit]
name = "MGit"
category = "navigation"
purpose = "Navigate related repositories"
rationale = "Keeps repository context visible"
platforms = ["macos"]
install.provider = "homebrew"
install.kind = "formula"
install.locator = "mgit"
install.platforms = ["macos"]

[profile.default]
name = "Default rig"
purpose = "Complete everyday setup"
kind = "complete"
.fi
.SH FILES
\&
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig/rig.toml
Optional root configuration file.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig/conf.d/*.toml
Optional configuration fragments.
.TP
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/rig
Default data directory.
.TP
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/rig/providers/ID
Conventional executable for an explicitly declared external provider whose
table omits
.BR executable .
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/rig
Default state directory.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/rig/last-upgrade
Report of the most recent unattended
.B rig upgrade
that dispatched work, replaced atomically and never written by a dry run. The
UTF-8 tab-separated text opens with
.B rig-last-run
and the integer report version, then one key and value line each for
.BR action ,
.BR profile ,
.BR platform ,
.BR finished ,
.BR status ,
.BR result ,
.BR detail ,
and
.BR summary ,
then the target, provider, result, and detail header and the same rows the run
printed. It is a stable contract for a wrapper that notifies or reports, and
carries no path, locator, argument, credential, or native output. Rig does not
itself notify.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/rig/resources/PLATFORM.tsv
Atomic reconciliation receipt for resources last successfully managed on one
platform. Each tab-separated row stores only provider, resource kind, identity,
and locator; it is not observed state or declaration authority. A row survives
a run that did not select it, and becomes retirement work only once the
catalogue stops declaring it for the platform.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/rig/apply-history/PLATFORM.tsv
Bounded versioned apply-outcome ledger keyed by qualified declared target and
provider or skill authority. Each completed attempt stores an ordering token,
UTC completion time and native exit status, never arguments, locators or
provider output. Internal successful records prevent an older concurrent
failure from replacing a newer completed success. Short write locks never
span native execution. Each completed result is published immediately;
interrupted in-flight work publishes nothing. Dry runs and read-only commands
do not write, prune or repair history. Removed declarations are pruned only
on real history writes; profile deselection alone does not remove evidence.
The ledger is limited to 4 MiB and 4096 rows, with no age-based expiry.
Persistence warnings do not rewrite provider outcomes or apply exit status.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/rig/reconciliation/PLATFORM.lock
Exclusive live reconciliation lock. Rig acquires it before reading a receipt
and holds it through provider work and atomic receipt replacement. Existing
active, stale, or unknown owners are reported and not removed automatically.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/rig
Default cache directory.
.SH PUBLIC DATA EXPORT
\&
.PP
The
.B rig export
writes exactly one regular, non-symlink
.I rig.json
file. Its top-level
.B format
is
.B rig-publication
and its integer
.B version
is 1. It contains export metadata, profile identity, selected categories,
and selected tool identity, name, purpose, rationale, declared platforms, and
closed relationships. It always includes a deterministic profile skills array,
including when empty. A selected skill contributes only identity, name, purpose,
rationale, and optional reviewed public source. Skill authority, native source,
runtime projections, paths, roots, locks, arguments, observed state, and
unmanaged inventory are never rendered. Category, tool, skill, platform, and
relationship values use
deterministic bytewise ordering.
.PP
The exported profile must be a non-appliable view and is resolved independently
of the active host platform while each tool retains its declared platforms.
Every selected declaration and dependency must opt into the view explicitly.
Relationships are included only when both endpoints are selected. Provider
configuration and tool installation metadata, private profiles, observed state,
credentials, and machine paths are not rendered.
Consumers should check both
.B format
and
.B version
before interpreting the remaining document.
.PP
Rig first renders a complete sibling staging tree and then replaces the named
output directory. It never merges with an existing tree. For safety, Rig
rejects output targets that are
.BR / ,
.BR . ,
.BR .. ,
symlinks, or non-directories. Review the generated tree before passing it to a
separate hosting system. Rig does not deploy: take the tree where it belongs
using the credentials and transport that system already has.
.SH PROVIDER EXECUTION
\&
.PP
Built-in adapters map selected tool installations to native commands. Homebrew
.B formula
and
.B cask
installations use
.BR brew (1);
.B mas
installations use
.BR mas (1).
The
.B uv
adapter supports
.B tool
installations, the
.B mise
adapter supports
.B tool
installations, the
.B npm
adapter supports
.B global
installations, and the
.B chezmoi
adapter supports
.B target
installations. Their native observation and application commands are
respectively:
.PP
.nf
brew list --formula|--cask --versions LOCATOR
brew install --formula|--cask LOCATOR
mas list
mas install LOCATOR
uv tool list
uv tool install LOCATOR
mise which LOCATOR
mise install LOCATOR
npm list --global --depth=0 LOCATOR
npm install --global LOCATOR
chezmoi status --path-style=absolute -- LOCATOR
chezmoi apply -- LOCATOR
.fi
.PP
No declaration is needed to enable these built-in identities or their fixed
operations. Optional configured provider arguments precede the native command.
Tool
.B install.arguments
precede the locator. Each configured value remains one literal argument. An
allowed explicit
.B executable
replaces the adapter default.
.PP
A
.B direct-download
.B executable
installation requires an HTTPS locator, absolute destination, and lowercase
.B sha256:
checksum. Observation reads only the destination. Application uses
.BR curl (1)
to write a sibling temporary file, verifies SHA-256, sets executable mode, and
renames it over a regular destination. Rig refuses symlink and non-regular
destinations, restricts redirects to HTTPS, rechecks destination safety before
replacement, and removes failed temporary files.
.SS PROVIDER LIFECYCLE
\&
.PP
Built-in lifecycle capabilities are fixed by Rig. Package selection comes
from Rig declarations; no provider-native package manifest is read or
overwritten. External providers are not invoked by the upgrade command.
The internal capability remains named
.B update
even though the public verb is
.BR upgrade .
.PP
Selected upgrades use
.BR "brew upgrade" ,
.BR "mas upgrade" ,
.BR "uv tool upgrade" ,
.BR "mise upgrade" ,
or
.BR "npm install --global" .
Unsupported selected providers are report-only skips. Housekeeping belongs
to the native tools; apply and upgrade do not perform Bundle cleanup or delete
unknown software.
.PP
Homebrew capture inventory uses
.B brew list \-\-formula \-\-installed-on-request \-\-full-name
and
.BR "brew list \-\-cask \-\-full-name" .
It prepares reviewed Rig declarations rather than a Brewfile snapshot.
.PP
Apply and upgrade preflight their supported work before mutation and keep
native diagnostics on stderr. Capture is read-only except for explicitly
creating a new review file outside active configuration.
.SS DECLARATION LIFECYCLE
\&
.TS
allbox tab(|);
l l l.
Declaration|Selected lifecycle|Deselection
Tool|Observe, apply, upgrade|No automatic removal
Skill|Observe, apply, upgrade where supported|No automatic removal
Service or scheduled job|Observe, apply, receipt-backed retire|No retirement; deleting the declaration retires it
Setting or Dock layout|Observe and apply|No automatic reversal
Private port|Observe only|No mutation
.TE
.PP
Complete profiles may enter the mutating lifecycle. Views remain available for
queries and export but are rejected before mutation. Apply and upgrade report
selected declaration scope. Native execution may affect dependencies according
to provider semantics; Rig never widens selection to a whole Brewfile or uses
provider cleanup as part of reconciliation. Dry-run reports planned work
without provider mutation.
.SS BUILT-IN MACOS RESOURCES
\&
.PP
On macOS, the built-in
.B launchd
integration owns service and scheduled-job reconciliation without a provider
or action declaration. It renders one LaunchAgent property list beneath
.IR ~/Library/LaunchAgents ,
compares both its content and launchctl load state, replaces safe regular files,
loads or unloads the resolved user domain as declared, and removes stale
managed property lists during retirement. Other platforms report these
resources unavailable without mutation.
.PP
Use native launchctl and log tools for imperative status, restart, run, and log
operations. Rig's public surface owns declarative reconciliation rather than a
generic provider action dispatcher.
.PP
The built-in
.B macos-defaults
integration observes and applies typed settings. The built-in
.B macos-dock
integration observes and applies a declared semantic Dock order and explicitly
declared folder view and display attributes. Unspecified attributes remain
unconstrained. Observation compares full paths, never item labels, using one
native Dock snapshot when attribute evidence is needed. Proven order, view
and display mismatches take precedence in that order; incomplete attribute
evidence is unknown, not agreement. Observation never rewrites defaults or
restarts the Dock. Native macOS
application inventory is read-only. These integrations require neither
provider tables nor external executables and remain unavailable without
mutation on other platforms.
.SS EXTENSION PROVIDER PROTOCOL
\&
.PP
This protocol is an advanced extension-author interface, not part of ordinary
Rig configuration. An external provider declaration resolves one exact
executable, either from its
.B executable
field or the conventional Rig data path, and requires an exact
.B capabilities
array. Capability values are atomic and are not split on commas.
.PP
Rig invokes an external provider using this versioned literal-argument
protocol:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 VERB PROVIDER TOOL KIND LOCATOR [INSTALL_ARGUMENT ...]
.fi
.PP
.I VERB
is exactly
.B observe
or
.BR apply .
Rig inserts
.B rig-provider-v1
itself; a user never supplies that marker in configuration or a Rig command.
Built-in integrations use internal calls and never receive it.
Rig preserves every configured value as one argument and performs no
evaluation, shell expansion, or implicit word splitting.
.PP
External operational-resource providers use separate exact capabilities
.BR resource-observe ,
.BR resource-apply ,
and
.BR resource-retire .
Selected declarations use this protocol:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 observe-resource PROVIDER ID KIND LOCATOR [FIELD=VALUE ...]
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 apply-resource PROVIDER ID KIND LOCATOR [FIELD=VALUE ...]
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 retire-resource PROVIDER ID KIND LOCATOR previous-managed=true
.fi
.PP
Each scalar is one ordered
.I key=value
argument and each array value repeats its key in declaration order. Providers
receive complete resolved fields and do not parse Rig TOML.
Existing action declarations remain validated, but no public command dispatches
them; use native tools directly for imperative work.
.PP
Reverse reconciliation uses a separate fixed protocol that names no tool,
because it enumerates a provider's whole domain rather than observing one
declaration:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 inventory PROVIDER
.fi
.PP
An external provider must declare the exact
.B inventory
capability. Inventory stdout contains zero or more lines, each beginning with
an identity comparable to a tool installation
.BR locator ,
optionally followed by a tab and provider detail that Rig treats as opaque.
The separator is a tab because an identity may be a path containing spaces.
Rig reports every identity that no tool installation declares. Installation
metadata declares an identity either through its
.B locator
, matched only within that installation's own provider namespace because a
Mac App
Store numeric identity and a cask name may coincide, or through a
repeatable tool
.B artifact
value naming a machine-observable path the tool materialises, matched
whichever provider observed it. A
non-zero exit yields state
.B unknown
with detail
.BR exit:N .
Inventory is an observation and must not mutate the machine.
.PP
Observation stdout must contain exactly one of
.BR present ,
.BR missing ,
.BR drifted ,
.BR unavailable ,
or
.BR unknown .
Provider stderr remains diagnostic output. Invalid observation output becomes
.B unknown
with detail
.BR invalid-response ;
a non-zero provider exit becomes
.B unknown
with detail
.BR exit:N .
Application output is routed to stderr so Rig alone owns deterministic stdout.
.SH DEVELOPMENT INSTALLATIONS
\&
.PP
For development against a local checkout, run
.B ./install.sh --link
to link the current executable and manual into
.B RIG_INSTALL_DIR
(default
.IR ~/.local/bin )
and
.B RIG_MAN_INSTALL_DIR
(default: the XDG data directory beneath
.IR man/man1 ).
The linked command follows changes to the checkout and does not download a
release. Re-run link mode after moving the checkout. Use
.B ./bin/rig
directly or
.B man -l man/rig.1
to inspect the checked-out artifacts without changing installed paths.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\&
.PP
Rig owns the following statuses. Interrupted commands return signal statuses.
.TP
.B 0
Healthy status or doctor result, successful init, capture, apply, upgrade,
query, or export, including neutral catalogue-only rows and unsupported
lifecycle skips.
.TP
.B 1
Unhealthy observation or doctor finding; unavailable target, provider runtime
failure, dependency suppression, or failed native capture inventory.
Doctor also returns 1 when it cannot load configuration.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid syntax, unknown identity, invalid configuration, resolution failure,
unsafe output path, missing capture intent metadata for an output request,
or shared preflight safety failure.
.TP
.B 129, 130, 143
Interrupted by HUP, INT, or TERM.
.PP
Unless
.B RIG_OUTCOME
suppresses it, the command reports its outcome and exit status on its final
stderr line.
